    "result_text": "AcceptanceTest is the only test class and it passes. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on the create-owner endpoint \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) that share the new owner's first and last name, compared case-insensitively.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions and mirroring the existing `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` pattern:\n\n1. **`AssignOwnerNamesakeCount`** (new function class) \u2014 the single piece of new logic. Given the built `Owner`, it counts existing owners matching both first and last name case-insensitively (filtering the `findByLastName` prefix results down to exact matches) and stores it via `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)`. It runs after `BuildOwner` and before `SaveOwner`, so the owner being created is never counted among its own namesakes.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `assignNamesakeCount` step into the pipeline between `build` and `assignCustomerCode`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n3. **`Owner` entity** \u2014 added a persisted `namesake_count` column with getter/setter, so the value is stored at creation and returned on later `GET /api/owners/{id}` (which the test relies on).\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer to the `Owner` schema; the generated `OwnerDto` and MapStruct's `OwnerMapper` pick it up automatically by name (like `customerCode`/`householdId`).\n\n5. **Schema files** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column so all supported databases match the entity.\n\nThe counting logic stays in its own small single-responsibility class rather than being bolted onto an existing step, and reuses the repository's `findByLastName` lookup rather than scanning all owners.",
